<PageLayout
  title="Complexity Is Not Depth — AnyBody Can Design"
  description="Why adding more features rarely adds more value, and the discipline it takes to simplify without losing meaning."
  label="AnyBody Can Design — Jun 2024"
  heading="Complexity Is Not Depth"
  subheading="On what happens when you confuse more with better."
>
  <article class="case-study" aria-label="Article">
    <div class="container case-study-grid">
      <div class="case-main">
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Complexity arrives without being invited. A product begins with one clear thing it does well. Then someone asks for a second thing. Then a third. Then integration with something else. Each addition feels justified at the time. Each one has a reason. But at some point, the product stops being the thing it was and becomes a collection of reasons instead.</p>

          <h2>The confusion at the root of it</h2>
          <p>There is a common assumption that complexity signals depth — that a thing with more features, more options, more settings is somehow more serious or more capable than a thing with fewer. This is wrong in a way that is worth examining carefully, because the wrongness is subtle.</p>

          <p>Depth is about what a product can help you do. Complexity is about how much you have to navigate to do it. These are not the same thing. A product can be deeply capable and simple to use. A product can be enormously complicated and still only do one mediocre thing.</p>

          <blockquote>Simplicity is not the absence of thought. It is the result of more of it.</blockquote>

          <h2>The discipline of removing</h2>
          <p>Adding features is easy. Every feature has an advocate — someone for whom it would be genuinely useful. The discipline is in asking whether its presence harms anyone else, and whether the product as a whole is better or worse for including it.</p>

          <p>Most teams are good at deciding whether a feature should be built. Far fewer are good at deciding whether a feature should remain — whether it is still earning its place given what the product has become around it.</p>

          <h2>Simplifying without losing meaning</h2>
          <p>Simplification is not reduction for its own sake. There is a version of simplicity that is just subtraction — removing things because they feel extraneous, without understanding what they were carrying. This produces products that are clean but thin.</p>

          <p>Genuine simplification asks what each part is doing — not what it is, but what it is carrying — and then finds a way to carry the same weight with less structure. That is harder. It requires understanding what actually matters to the person using the thing.</p>

          <p>The test for whether you have succeeded is not whether the product looks simple. It is whether the person using it feels clear.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
